Summary
This solution uses a checkbox hack for the mobile menu toggle, allowing it to remain CSS-only without requiring JavaScript.

HTML Structure
<nav class="navbar">
  <div class="logo">Logo</div>
  <input type="checkbox" id="click">
  <label for="click" class="menu-btn">&#9776;</label>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="#">Home</a></li>
    <li><a href="#">Services</a></li>
    <li><a href="#">Portfolio</a></li>
    <li><a href="#">Contact</a></li>
  </ul>
</nav>

CSS Code
* { margin: 0; padding: 0; box-sizing: border-box; }
.navbar { display: flex; height: 80px; width: 100%; background: #333; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between; padding: 0 50px; color: #fff; }
.navbar ul { display: flex; list-style: none; }
.navbar ul li { margin: 0 5px; }
.navbar ul li a { color: #fff; text-decoration: none; font-size: 18px; padding: 8px 15px; }
.menu-btn, #click { display: none; }

@media (max-width: 768px) {
  .menu-btn { display: block; font-size: 30px; cursor: pointer; }
  .navbar ul { position: fixed; top: 80px; left: -100%; background: #111; height: 100vh; width: 100%; display: block; text-align: center; transition: all 0.3s ease; }
  #click:checked ~ ul { left: 0; }
  .navbar ul li { margin: 40px 0; }
  .navbar ul li a { font-size: 20px; }
}

